latest_plan = "Based on our reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Extract the Border and Initial Structure:\n   - Copy the left 6 columns of the input grid to the output grid.\n   - Identify the border color (usually the color of the top-left pixel).\n\n2. Identify Shapes on the Right Side:\n   - Scan columns 7-12 of the input grid.\n   - Use a flood fill or connected component algorithm to identify distinct shapes.\n   - Store each shape as a list of coordinates along with its color.\n\n3. Process the Identified Shapes:\n   - For each shape:\n     a. Invert it vertically (flip top to bottom).\n     b. Calculate its dimensions (height and width).\n     c. Calculate its area (height * width).\n   - Sort the processed shapes by area in descending order.\n\n4. Identify Empty Spaces within the Border:\n   - Scan the left 6 columns of the output grid.\n   - Identify contiguous empty spaces (areas not filled with the border color).\n   - Merge vertically adjacent empty spaces.\n   - Calculate the dimensions and area of each empty space.\n   - Sort empty spaces by area in descending order.\n\n5. Place Inverted Shapes in Empty Spaces:\n   - Iterate through the sorted empty spaces.\n   - For each empty space, find the largest shape that fits.\n   - Place the shape in the empty space, maintaining its vertical orientation.\n   - Remove the placed shape from the list of shapes to be placed.\n\n6. Fill Remaining Empty Spaces:\n   - Scan the output grid for any remaining empty spaces (color 0).\n   - Fill these spaces with the border color.\n\n7. Verify the Output:\n   - Ensure the output grid has 6 columns.\n   - Check that the border structure is preserved.\n   - Confirm that all shapes from the right side of the input are present in the output.\n\n8. Return the Transformed Grid:\n   - Return the 6-column output grid as the solution.\n\nImplementation Notes:\n- Use the ColoredGrid class methods where applicable (e.g., get_dimensions, find_connected_regions).\n- Implement helper functions for tasks like inverting shapes and identifying empty spaces.\n- Use efficient data structures (e.g., sets for quick lookups) where appropriate.\n- Handle edge cases, such as when there are more shapes than available empty spaces.\n\nThis plan follows the general idea we identified and should work for various inputs that follow the pattern observed in the test cases. It preserves the border, efficiently utilizes space, and maintains the relative positioning of shapes while allowing for necessary rearrangement."
